From 80856b4174c6f86f0c6ceec2193a3c846e16c70d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Hargett <plaztiksyke@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 16 May 2026 17:01:53 -0700
Subject: pulley: trap on null in 8 fused funcref-dispatch handlers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Codex review on the rebeckerspecialties wasmtime fork PR pointed
out that phase-2/3's load absorption breaks the slow path's
correctness, not just performance.

The IR shape (`func_environ.rs`):

  predecessor:
    value        = load (table + 0)
    value_masked = band value, -2
    brif value_masked, continuation([value_masked]), null_block([])

  null_block:
    returned_entry = call lazy_init_funcref(vmctx, table_idx, index)
    jump continuation([returned_entry])

  continuation(result_param):
    code  = load result_param + offset_code
    vmctx = load result_param + offset_vmctx
    call_indirect code, vmctx, ...

Phase 2's `FuncrefDispatch` (and phase 3's `BandFuncrefDispatch`)
absorb the two loads from `continuation` via `sink_pure_inst` into
the brif-replacing fused op. The fused op writes `dst_code` and
`dst_vmctx` ONLY on the non-null side. The null side previously
fell through (or branched, in the "_not" variants) to
`null_block`'s lazy-init libcall, which then jumped back to
`continuation`. But `continuation`'s loads are gone — so
`call_indirect` would see uninitialized `dst_code` / `dst_vmctx`.

The fusion is gated on `is_eagerly_initialized_funcref_table`,
which guarantees the slot is never null at runtime, so the slow
path is unreachable in correct code. The previous handler's
`ControlFlow::Continue(())` on null was billed as "defence-in-depth
in case the predicate is unsound" — but that defence-in-depth was
itself incorrect, because falling through reaches `null_block →
continuation` with the same broken register state.

Fix: in all 8 fused-dispatch handlers (4 forward + 4 "_not"
variants across x64/x32 × xfuncref_dispatch/xband_funcref_dispatch),
replace the null fall-through / null branch with `done_trap`.
Soundness:

- Under the predicate (correct code): `src != 0` at runtime, the
  fast path always fires, the trap is unreachable. No behavioural
  change.
- Under a hypothetical predicate failure: the handler traps with
  a clean error at the fused op's PC, instead of running the slow
  path's `call_indirect` with garbage. Fails closed.

The `offset` field on the "_not" variants becomes vestigial after
the fix — kept in the encoding for shape parity with the forward
variants; silenced with `let _ = offset;` to suppress unused-var
warnings.

Tests: 2237/2237 filetests pass (no snapshots change — the trap is
runtime-only). 13/13 `craneliftpulley_call_*` integration tests
pass. 1 pulley-interpreter test (test_call_indirect_host_width).
